.Em propq ]
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command processes Netscape signed public key and challenge (SPKAC)
files.
It can print out their contents, verify the signature and produce its
own SPKACs from a supplied private key.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -in Em filename
This specifies the input filename to read from or standard input if this
option is not specified.
Ignored if the
.Sy -key
option is used.
.It Sy -out Em filename
Specifies the output filename to write to or standard output by default.
.It Sy -digest Em digest
Use the specified
.Em digest
to sign a created SPKAC file.
The default digest algorithm is MD5.
.It Sy -key Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
Create an SPKAC file using the private key specified by
.Em filename
or
.Em uri .
The
.Sy -in ,
.Sy -noout ,
.Sy -spksect
and
.Sy -verify
options are ignored if present.
.It Sy -keyform Sy DER Ns | Ns Sy PEM Ns | Ns Sy P12 Ns | Ns Sy ENGINE
The key format; unspecified by default.
See
.Xr openssl-format-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The input file password source.
For more information about the format of
.Em arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -challenge Em string
Specifies the challenge string if an SPKAC is being created.
.It Sy -spkac Em spkacname
Allows an alternative name form the variable containing the SPKAC. The
default is "SPKAC". This option affects both generated and input SPKAC
files.
.It Sy -spksect Em section
Allows an alternative name form the section containing the SPKAC. The
default is the default section.
.It Sy -noout
Don't output the text version of the SPKAC (not used if an SPKAC is
being created).
.It Sy -pubkey
Output the public key of an SPKAC (not used if an SPKAC is being
created).
.It Sy -verify
Verifies the digital signature on the supplied SPKAC.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Print out the contents of an SPKAC:
.Bd -literal
 openssl spkac -in spkac.cnf
.Ed
.Pp
Verify the signature of an SPKAC:
.Bd -literal
 openssl spkac -in spkac.cnf -noout -verify
.Ed
.Pp
Create an SPKAC using the challenge string "hello":
.Bd -literal
 openssl spkac -key key.pem -challenge hello -out spkac.cnf
.Ed
.Pp
Example of an SPKAC, (long lines split up for clarity):
.Bd -literal
 SPKAC=MIG5MGUwXDANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQEFAANLADBIAkEA\e
 1cCoq2Wa3Ixs47uI7FPVwHVIPDx5yso105Y6zpozam135a\e
 8R0CpoRvkkigIyXfcCjiVi5oWk+6FfPaD03uPFoQIDAQAB\e
 FgVoZWxsbzANBgkqhkiG9w0BAQQFAANBAFpQtY/FojdwkJ\e
 h1bEIYuc2EeM2KHTWPEepWYeawvHD0gQ3DngSC75YCWnnD\e
 dq+NQ3F+X4deMx9AaEglZtULwV4=
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
A created SPKAC with suitable DN components appended can be fed to
.Xr openssl-ca 1 .
.Pp
SPKACs are typically generated by Netscape when a form is submitted
containing the
.Sy KEYGEN
tag as part of the certificate enrollment process.
.Pp
The challenge string permits a primitive form of proof of possession of
private key.
By checking the SPKAC signature and a random challenge string some
guarantee is given that the user knows the private key corresponding to
the public key being certified.
This is important in some applications.
Without this it is possible for a previous SPKAC to be used in a "replay
attack".
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr openssl-ca 1
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Sy -engine
option was deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0.
.Pp
The
.Sy -digest
option was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
